# Cloudflare Pages — response headers
#
# The repository root is the Pages output directory (there is no build step),
# so this file ships as-is.
#
# WHY THESE PAGES MUST NOT BE INDEXED
#
# index.html is the application and is public by intent. The other four are
# operational surfaces: probe.html transmits live metering from a facility,
# control.html issues remote commands to a probe (Connect, Reset Integration,
# Get State) with no authentication in front of it, wallboard.html renders a
# gallery view of whatever probes are reporting, and tools/ holds a diagnostic.
#
# None of them should appear in a search result. A wallboard indexed under a
# facility's name discloses that the facility exists, what it is called and
# what its loudness looked like when the crawler passed. That is a client's
# information, not ours to publish.
#
# This is disclosure control, not access control. It asks well-behaved crawlers
# not to list the pages; it stops nobody who has the URL. Real gating needs a
# Cloudflare Access policy on these paths, which is a dashboard change and is
# recorded as outstanding in the estate doctrine.
#
# The header survives the tsg-proxy Worker, which forwards origin headers when
# serving these pages under the brand path, so one declaration covers both the
# pages.dev address and the proxied path.

/probe.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow, noarchive, nosnippet
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

/control.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow, noarchive, nosnippet
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

/wallboard.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow, noarchive, nosnippet
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

/tools/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow, noarchive, nosnippet
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
